    <article>
      <section class="hero shell"><div><p class="eyebrow">Independent Technical Prototype · 2025</p><h1>Pressure–Inertial Sleep-Posture Detection and Actuation Prototype</h1><p class="lede">A five-channel benchtop system integrating distributed pressure sensing, inertial orientation estimation, conditional state detection, and servo actuation.</p><div class="chips"><span>5 × FSR</span><span>ADXL345</span><span>5 × servo</span><span>Sensor fusion</span></div></div><figure class="hero__visual"><img :src="asset('final-five-channel-system.jpg')" alt="Final five-FSR and five-servo sleep-posture prototype" /><figcaption>Final physical configuration · 5 sensing and 5 actuation channels</figcaption></figure></section>

      <section class="objective"><div class="shell split"><div><p class="kicker">Technical objective</p><h2>Require both local pressure and body orientation before actuation</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>I developed the sensing, wiring, Arduino logic, and actuation tests as a staged technical prototype. Pressure alone can indicate contact but not body orientation; inertial data alone can indicate tilt but not where load is applied. The control concept therefore combines both modalities before triggering a response.</p><p>The current evidence demonstrates benchtop sensing and actuator control, not a clinically validated sleep intervention or finished wearable.</p></div></div></section>

      <section class="pipeline shell"><div class="section-intro"><p class="kicker">Final physical configuration</p><h2>Five sensing locations mapped to five actuator channels</h2></div><div class="flow"><article><span>01</span><strong>5-point pressure array</strong><p>Distributed FSR inputs capture local contact and loading.</p></article><i>+</i><article><span>02</span><strong>ADXL345 orientation</strong><p>Three-axis acceleration is converted into pitch and roll.</p></article><i>→</i><article><span>03</span><strong>Conditional trigger</strong><p>Pressure state and orientation state must both be active.</p></article><i>→</i><article><span>04</span><strong>5-channel actuation</strong><p>Five physical servo channels demonstrate localized outputs.</p></article></div><div class="iteration-note"><strong>Development record</strong><p>The surviving integrated Arduino file documents an earlier four-FSR/four-servo iteration, created when only four pressure sensors were available. The later physical integration shown in the final project evidence expanded the system to five sensing and five actuation channels.</p></div></section>

      <section class="development"><div class="shell"><div class="section-intro"><p class="kicker">Staged implementation</p><h2>The system was built from isolated sensor tests to multimodal integration</h2></div><div class="stages"><article><span>01</span><strong>Single-FSR calibration</strong><p>Mapped one analog pressure input into a normalized serial curve.</p></article><article><span>02</span><strong>Five-point acquisition</strong><p>Expanded the pressure test to five simultaneous FSR signals.</p></article><article><span>03</span><strong>Orientation sensing</strong><p>Read ADXL345 acceleration and computed pitch/roll estimates.</p></article><article><span>04</span><strong>Fusion and actuation</strong><p>Combined pressure, tilt, trigger state, and timed servo response.</p></article></div></div></section>

      <section class="evidence shell"><div class="section-intro"><p class="kicker">Physical evidence</p><h2>Hardware integration and serial-state inspection</h2></div><div class="evidence-grid"><figure><img :src="asset('fsr-array.jpg')" alt="Pressure sensors connected to the Arduino development circuit" /><figcaption>Distributed FSR sensing hardware</figcaption></figure><figure><img :src="asset('serial-output.jpg')" alt="Arduino serial output showing pressure, pitch, and roll data" /><figcaption>Pressure channels with pitch / roll output</figcaption></figure></div></section>

      <section class="demo"><div class="shell"><div class="section-intro"><p class="kicker">Integrated demonstration</p><h2>Pressure input produces a visible multi-servo response</h2><p class="prose">The video records the benchtop integration test. It demonstrates sensing and actuation behavior; the servos are intentionally left exposed so the output channels remain inspectable.</p></div><figure><video controls muted playsinline preload="metadata" :poster="asset('integration-demo-poster.jpg')"><source :src="media('integration-test.m4v')" type="video/mp4" /></video><figcaption>Benchtop integration test · real hardware and programmed response</figcaption></figure></div></section>

      <section class="logic shell"><div><p class="kicker">Code-derived control safeguards</p><h2>Trigger state was stabilized before the actuator command</h2></div><div class="logic-list"><article><strong>Dual condition</strong><p>The documented iteration triggers only when pressure exceeds its threshold and <code>|pitch|</code> or <code>|roll|</code> exceeds 10°.</p></article><article><strong>Hysteresis</strong><p>A 50-unit release margin prevents pressure noise close to the threshold from repeatedly changing state.</p></article><article><strong>Edge triggering</strong><p>A latched pressed-state prevents continuous re-triggering while the sensor remains loaded.</p></article><article><strong>Non-blocking timing</strong><p><code>millis()</code>-based timing stops each servo after its programmed motion interval without freezing the other channels.</p></article></div></section>

      <section class="boundary"><div class="shell boundary__inner"><strong>Evidence boundary</strong><p>This page documents a five-channel physical configuration and a recoverable four-channel code iteration. It does not claim automated posture classification accuracy, overnight testing, scoliosis correction, sleep-quality improvement, or results from a clinical/user study.</p></div></section>
